275 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 107 0 R
/T (Economic theory assumes exchange, that something is given, something is returned, individuals participate voluntarily, each maximising their own utility. This assumes the possibility of refusal, and where refusal carries cost, be it social, economic, relational, then participation is no longer voluntary, it is structured. Within the family, this becomes even more pronounced. The household is often treated as a unit with shared interests, a space of cooperation rather than conflict, but as shows, this assumption collapses once we recognise that individuals within households do not have equal access to resources, and therefore not equal bargaining power.)
/E (Economic theory assumes exchange, that something is given, something is returned, individuals participate voluntarily, each maximising their own utility. This assumes the possibility of refusal, and where refusal carries cost, be it social, economic, relational, then participation is no longer voluntary, it is structured. Within the family, this becomes even more pronounced. The household is often treated as a unit with shared interests, a space of cooperation rather than conflict, but as shows, this assumption collapses once we recognise that individuals within households do not have equal access to resources, and therefore not equal bargaining power.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
