1114 0 obj
<<
/Title (In Central Australian communities such as the Dieri, the acquisition of goods like red ochre, which is used for ceremonial and symbolic purposes, required long-distance expeditions to regions where it was naturally available. Infact, it was such long-distance trade, or rather external trade, which came to being first, and then further gave rise to internal trade.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1096 0 R
/Prev 1108 0 R
/Next 1118 0 R
>>
endobj
1115 0 obj
<<
/Title (A different but equally structured form of exchange can be observed in the Kula ring of the Trobriand Islands. In this system, shell necklaces \(soulava\) and armbands \(mwali\) was traded across a network of islands in fixed directional patterns.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1096 0 R
/Prev 1119 0 R
/Next 1109 0 R
>>
endobj
1116 0 obj
<<
/Title (There is a pattern in such instances. Exchange exists, but it is not organised through markets. It is embedded within social arrangements by the ways of territory, hierarchy, kinship, and obligation. Production and distribution are not separate economic processes, they are regulated through norms, authority, and collective structures that define who can access what, and under what conditions. Also the mere fact, that a tribe which used money differentiated it very little economically from other tribes on the same cultural level, who did not, shows how markets and their regulation through prices is constructed and is definately not a natural extension of human exchange, which is embedded in social relations and reciprocation.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1110 0 R
/Next 1120 0 R
>>
endobj
