1141 0 obj
<<
/Title (Economic theory assumes exchange, that something is given, something is returned, individuals participate voluntarily, each maximising their own utility. This assumes the possibility of refusal, and where refusal carries cost, be it social, economic, relational, then participation is no longer voluntary, it is structured. Within the family, this becomes even more pronounced. The household is often treated as a unit with shared interests, a space of cooperation rather than conflict, but as shows, this assumption collapses once we recognise that individuals within households do not have equal access to resources, and therefore not equal bargaining power.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1132 0 R
/Next 1142 0 R
>>
endobj
1142 0 obj
<<
/Title (So what appears as harmony is often the absence of visible conflict, not the absence of inequality, and what appears as reciprocity is often sustained expectation \(which is not the same thing\). The model calls it exchange; the structure enforces it as obligation.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1132 0 R
/Prev 1141 0 R
>>
endobj
1143 0 obj
<<
/Title (From a distance, it can be described in familiar economic terms a supply, demand and pricing, contribution to local economies.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1124 0 R
/Prev 1136 0 R
/Next 1148 0 R
>>
endobj
1144 0 obj
<<
/Title (Within Kamathipura, work is organised through layered systems of dependence. Brothels, intermediaries, and informal protection networks regulate access to clients, housing, and income. Earnings move across these layers. Participation becomes possible while exit becomes difficult.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1124 0 R
/Prev 1149 0 R
/Next 1137 0 R
>>
endobj
